{"@odata.actions":[{"hint":"Fill in the direction proposal. Self-loop on Proposed.","name":"Propose","target":"Directions('en-019d9565-d200-7472-bfc9-4fa49bdf1f5e')/Temper.Propose"},{"hint":"Submit the direction for peer review.","name":"SubmitForReview","target":"Directions('en-019d9565-d200-7472-bfc9-4fa49bdf1f5e')/Temper.SubmitForReview"},{"hint":"Archive a direction at any pre-selected stage.","name":"Archive","target":"Directions('en-019d9565-d200-7472-bfc9-4fa49bdf1f5e')/Temper.Archive"}],"@odata.children":{},"@odata.context":"$metadata#Directions/$entity","@odata.id":"Directions('en-019d9565-d200-7472-bfc9-4fa49bdf1f5e')","booleans":{},"counters":{},"entity_id":"en-019d9565-d200-7472-bfc9-4fa49bdf1f5e","entity_type":"Direction","events":[{"action":"Created","from_status":"","params":{},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:26:11.329930Z","to_status":"Proposed"},{"action":"Propose","from_status":"Proposed","params":{"counterfactual_summary":"If this direction is wrong, model quality and product usability will be sufficient to drive broad adoption without major organizational restructuring or workforce-management intervention.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d9565-5930-7530-b4ec-d6a35403486e","en-019d9565-7a81-79c0-89f1-5a1d81469526"]","projection_id":"en-019d9564-d1e4-7b90-a9c7-b851c26d4945","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d9564-d26d-7722-97af-cfd50dfb8547","reasoning":"The deeper adoption constraint over the next 180-365 days is organizational, not purely technical. Directed software evolution asks firms to reassign responsibility across developers, platform teams, QA, security, and managers, yet most organizations still reward local control and clear authorship more than system-level experimentation. That means even as model quality improves, adoption will remain shallow unless firms redesign operating norms: who approves generated variants, who owns regression risk, how evaluation budgets are funded, and how teams are measured when output is produced with agent assistance. Enterprises that fail to answer those questions will see agents used opportunistically by enthusiasts while the broader organization stalls.

The labor dimension amplifies this problem. When employees suspect coding agents are a headcount-reduction tool, they become less willing to share workflow data, standardize practices, or trust agent-generated changes. Managers may also resist because the tools compress supervision layers and make some coordination work less valuable. So the adoption winners in this horizon will be organizations that explicitly frame agents as a redesign of team structure and incentive systems, not just a developer productivity plugin. Those that do not will likely experience a plateau in production adoption even if individual developers continue to use the tools privately.","step_at":"1","title":"Organizational/Adoption: Coding-agent rollout plateaus unless firms redesign incentives, accountability, and workforce messaging"},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:26:11.334717Z","to_status":"Proposed"}],"fields":{"Id":"en-019d9565-d200-7472-bfc9-4fa49bdf1f5e","Status":"Proposed","counterfactual_summary":"If this direction is wrong, model quality and product usability will be sufficient to drive broad adoption without major organizational restructuring or workforce-management intervention.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d9565-5930-7530-b4ec-d6a35403486e","en-019d9565-7a81-79c0-89f1-5a1d81469526"]","projection_id":"en-019d9564-d1e4-7b90-a9c7-b851c26d4945","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d9564-d26d-7722-97af-cfd50dfb8547","reasoning":"The deeper adoption constraint over the next 180-365 days is organizational, not purely technical. Directed software evolution asks firms to reassign responsibility across developers, platform teams, QA, security, and managers, yet most organizations still reward local control and clear authorship more than system-level experimentation. That means even as model quality improves, adoption will remain shallow unless firms redesign operating norms: who approves generated variants, who owns regression risk, how evaluation budgets are funded, and how teams are me