{"@odata.actions":[{"hint":"Fill in the direction proposal. Self-loop on Proposed.","name":"Propose","target":"Directions('en-019d9565-d6b8-7b13-9d6a-2630567a649e')/Temper.Propose"},{"hint":"Submit the direction for peer review.","name":"SubmitForReview","target":"Directions('en-019d9565-d6b8-7b13-9d6a-2630567a649e')/Temper.SubmitForReview"},{"hint":"Archive a direction at any pre-selected stage.","name":"Archive","target":"Directions('en-019d9565-d6b8-7b13-9d6a-2630567a649e')/Temper.Archive"}],"@odata.children":{},"@odata.context":"$metadata#Directions/$entity","@odata.id":"Directions('en-019d9565-d6b8-7b13-9d6a-2630567a649e')","booleans":{},"counters":{},"entity_id":"en-019d9565-d6b8-7b13-9d6a-2630567a649e","entity_type":"Direction","events":[{"action":"Created","from_status":"","params":{},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:26:12.537442Z","to_status":"Proposed"},{"action":"Propose","from_status":"Proposed","params":{"counterfactual_summary":"If teams do not adopt repo-specific staged evaluation, agent rollout decisions will continue to rely on weak proxies, causing costly false confidence and preventing disciplined evolutionary improvement.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d9565-42b5-7e03-8289-3d9bd183e683","en-019d9565-5c1d-76a1-9148-ca12030c891e"]","projection_id":"en-019d9564-d1e4-7b90-a9c7-b851c26d4945","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d9564-d23b-7e30-8f66-cab6fe3e8895","reasoning":"Evaluation/testing direction: repository-specific harnesses will replace generic coding benchmarks as the gating layer for agent rollout. Public benchmarks are useful for model selection, but they do not capture the realities that determine whether an agent can safely evolve a live system: flaky integration tests, hidden architectural constraints, migration ordering, and local code review norms. As a result, mature teams will build evaluation loops that replay real tickets against sampled historical tasks, score candidate diffs on pass-rate and rework metrics, and only promote agents that clear internal thresholds over repeated runs.

This shift also changes the economics of experimentation. Once evaluation is staged and cached, teams can afford multi-candidate search because weak candidates are eliminated by static checks and focused unit suites before expensive end-to-end validation. That makes selection pressure explicit: the system keeps variants that improve measurable repo outcomes rather than variants that merely look plausible in chat. In practice, the engineering organizations that move first will treat evaluation harnesses as product infrastructure with dedicated ownership, versioning, and observability, similar to how serious ML teams treat offline and online eval pipelines.","step_at":"1","title":"Evaluation/testing direction: repo-specific staged harnesses become the deployment gate for coding agents"},"timestamp":"2026-04-16T08:26:12.540824Z","to_status":"Proposed"}],"fields":{"Id":"en-019d9565-d6b8-7b13-9d6a-2630567a649e","Status":"Proposed","counterfactual_summary":"If teams do not adopt repo-specific staged evaluation, agent rollout decisions will continue to rely on weak proxies, causing costly false confidence and preventing disciplined evolutionary improvement.","grounding":"[]","observation_ids":"["en-019d9565-42b5-7e03-8289-3d9bd183e683","en-019d9565-5c1d-76a1-9148-ca12030c891e"]","projection_id":"en-019d9564-d1e4-7b90-a9c7-b851c26d4945","proposer_agent_id":"aj-019d9564-d23b-7e30-8f66-cab6fe3e8895","reasoning":"Evaluation/testing direction: repository-specific harnesses will replace generic coding benchmarks as the gating layer for agent rollout. Public benchmarks are useful for model selection, but they do not capture the realities that determine whether an agent can safely evolve a live system: flaky integration tests, hidden architectural constraints, migration ordering, and local code review norms. As a result, mature teams will build evaluation loops that replay real tickets against sampled historical tasks, score candidate diffs on pass-rate and rework metrics, and only promote agents that clear internal thresholds over repeated runs.

This shift also changes the economics of experimentation. Once evaluation is staged and cached, teams can afford multi-candidate search because weak candidates are eliminated by static checks and focused unit suites before expensive end-to-end validation. That makes selection pressure explicit: the system keeps variants that improve measurable repo outcomes rather than variants that merely look plausible in chat. In practice, the engineering organizations that move first will treat evaluation harnesses as product infrastructure with dedicated ownership, versioning, and observability, similar to how serious ML teams treat offline and online eval pipelines.","step_at":"1","title":"Evaluation/testing direction: repo-specific staged harnesses become the deployment gate for coding agents"},"item_count":0,"lists":{},"sequence_nr":2,"status":"Proposed","total_event_count":2}

